For your weather app, a small `localStorage` cache keeps the solution dependency-free and helps conserve your 1,000-call daily quota. This example caches each city’s response for **one hour**; use a shorter duration if fresher weather matters more.

```javascript
const CACHE_PREFIX = "weather:";
const CACHE_TTL_MS = 60 * 60 * 1000; // 1 hour

async function getWeather(city) {
  const normalizedCity = city.trim().toLowerCase();
  const cacheKey = CACHE_PREFIX + normalizedCity;

  // Return a fresh cached response when available.
  try {
    const saved = localStorage.getItem(cacheKey);
    if (saved) {
      const { data, timestamp } = JSON.parse(saved);
      if (Date.now() - timestamp < CACHE_TTL_MS) {
        return data;
      }
      localStorage.removeItem(cacheKey); // expired
    }
  } catch {
    // Ignore invalid cache data or unavailable storage; fetch normally.
  }

  const url =
    `https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather` +
    `?q=${encodeURIComponent(city.trim())}&appid=${API_KEY}`;

  const response = await fetch(url);
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Weather request failed: ${response.status}`);
  }

  const data = await response.json();

  try {
    localStorage.setItem(cacheKey, JSON.stringify({
      data,
      timestamp: Date.now()
    }));
  } catch {
    // Storage can be full or disabled; the fetched result still works.
  }

  return data;
}
```

Replace `API_KEY` with however your app currently supplies its key. With this setup, repeated searches for the same city use the cached response until it expires; after that, the next search refreshes it. A shorter TTL (for example, five minutes) gives fresher results but uses more API calls. Note that a key embedded in browser-side code is visible to users, so a server-side proxy is safer if your setup allows one.
